WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.057
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:04.057 --> 00:00:05.762
Building Non-Person Identity.

3
00:00:05.762 --> 00:00:09.639
This is Chapter 13: workload
identity across AWS

4
00:00:09.639 --> 00:00:13.663
GovCloud and Oracle Cloud
Infrastructure, or O C I.

5
00:00:13.663 --> 00:00:18.608
A workload in AWS or O C I that holds
an access key is a service account with

6
00:00:18.608 --> 00:00:24.049
a password that never expires, in a console
nobody in identity governance opens.

7
00:00:24.049 --> 00:00:26.312
This chapter reads both clouds' exports

8
00:00:26.312 --> 00:00:28.712
with the same rules as
the rest of the track.

9
00:00:28.712 --> 00:00:31.586
We'll see what AWS,
Oracle and Microsoft say,

10
00:00:31.586 --> 00:00:36.678
what the kit adds to the vendors' own
reads, the inputs, the code and its checks,

11
00:00:36.678 --> 00:00:40.374
what's still open, the evidence,
two labs and the dangers.

12
00:00:40.374 --> 00:00:45.470
One note before we begin: the code is
illustrative, tested against fixtures.

13
00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:51.133
Why does this chapter exist?

14
00:00:51.133 --> 00:00:56.199
Because a workload in AWS or O C I that
holds a long-lived key is a service account

15
00:00:56.199 --> 00:00:58.481
with a password that never expires,

16
00:00:58.481 --> 00:01:01.708
in a console nobody in
identity governance opens.

17
00:01:01.708 --> 00:01:03.707
Both vendors point the same way.

18
00:01:03.707 --> 00:01:08.879
AWS says to require workloads to use
temporary credentials with IAM roles and,

19
00:01:08.879 --> 00:01:11.982
where possible, not to
create access keys at all.

20
00:01:11.982 --> 00:01:14.666
Oracle recommends instance principals,

21
00:01:14.666 --> 00:01:17.745
and rotating API keys
every ninety days or less.

22
00:01:17.745 --> 00:01:22.383
The program makes every long-lived credential
an exception with a replacement date.

23
00:01:22.383 --> 00:01:24.370
This chapter adds no new rule.

24
00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:27.833
It reads the guide's exports,
checks the rules the guide sets,

25
00:01:27.833 --> 00:01:29.980
and never touches either cloud.

26
00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:35.718
Here's what AWS says.

27
00:01:35.718 --> 00:01:40.130
The IAM credential report lists every
user, with its console password,

28
00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:43.668
its first two access keys
and its signing certificates.

29
00:01:43.668 --> 00:01:48.004
It leaves out service-specific
credentials, such as CodeCommit passwords,

30
00:01:48.004 --> 00:01:51.781
and any key beyond the first two,
so those need their own list calls.

31
00:01:51.781 --> 00:01:56.555
The role and user lists return no tags,
so the guide runs the tag reads too.

32
00:01:56.555 --> 00:01:58.543
GovCloud changes a few things.

33
00:01:58.543 --> 00:02:00.504
There's no global S T S endpoint,

34
00:02:00.504 --> 00:02:03.955
and no role between a GovCloud
and a standard account.

35
00:02:03.955 --> 00:02:09.539
Roles Anywhere works, but revocation through
the certificate's CRL address doesn't:

36
00:02:09.539 --> 00:02:14.880
AWS says to import your CRLs instead, and
it doesn't check an imported CRL's expiry.

37
00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:17.953
And trust policies should
never name everyone,

38
00:02:17.953 --> 00:02:21.405
and an O I D C role should
limit the token's subject.

39
00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:26.537
Oracle's side.

40
00:02:26.537 --> 00:02:29.061
A user can hold three API signing keys,

41
00:02:29.061 --> 00:02:34.125
and auth tokens and customer secret keys
never expire, two of each per user.

42
00:02:34.125 --> 00:02:38.537
The list commands return each credential's
identifier, never the secret.

43
00:02:38.537 --> 00:02:42.608
An instance principal's certificates
are created and rotated by O C I,

44
00:02:42.608 --> 00:02:46.250
but anyone who can sign in to the
instance acts with its rights;

45
00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:48.463
a function uses a resource principal.

46
00:02:48.463 --> 00:02:50.986
Both are authorized
through a dynamic group,

47
00:02:50.986 --> 00:02:53.713
which has no permissions
until a policy names it.

48
00:02:53.713 --> 00:02:56.413
In a policy, manage
means every permission.

49
00:02:56.413 --> 00:02:59.320
The program's tenancy is
in the O C two realm,

50
00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:02.062
so every IAM call goes to the home region.

51
00:03:02.062 --> 00:03:06.807
And Microsoft Learn says Entra
accepts tokens from workloads in AWS,

52
00:03:06.807 --> 00:03:10.137
only from issuers that sign
with R S two fifty-six.

53
00:03:10.137 --> 00:03:13.793
Whether GovCloud's tokens work
with Entra hasn't been tested,

54
00:03:13.793 --> 00:03:16.740
so the guide uses that
path only after a test.

55
00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:24.053
AWS and Oracle each give
a way to list what exists.

56
00:03:24.053 --> 00:03:27.624
AWS gives the credential
report, but it leaves out roles,

57
00:03:27.624 --> 00:03:32.742
keys beyond two and service-specific
credentials, and it names no key, so the kit

58
00:03:32.742 --> 00:03:37.702
reads the key lists too and joins each
key to a report column by creation date.

59
00:03:37.702 --> 00:03:39.715
That join is the kit's own design.

60
00:03:39.715 --> 00:03:41.998
The role list comes with trust policies,

61
00:03:41.998 --> 00:03:45.527
and the kit reads every principal
and condition into one file.

62
00:03:45.527 --> 00:03:49.245
AWS's Access Analyzer can report
unused access, for a charge,

63
00:03:49.245 --> 00:03:52.660
but the pages read don't say
whether it runs in GovCloud,

64
00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:57.289
so the kit reports keys unused for
ninety days from the credential report.

65
00:03:57.289 --> 00:04:01.607
On Oracle's side the kit makes one
list of every credential with its age,

66
00:04:01.607 --> 00:04:04.230
and joins policies to dynamic groups.

67
00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:11.842
The guide's export steps write the
files; the kit only reads them.

68
00:04:11.842 --> 00:04:14.315
On the AWS side: the credential report,

69
00:04:14.315 --> 00:04:17.734
every access key and
service-specific credential,

70
00:04:17.734 --> 00:04:21.478
the roles with their trust
policies, instance profiles,

71
00:04:21.478 --> 00:04:25.954
O I D C providers, and the Roles
Anywhere trust anchors and profiles.

72
00:04:25.954 --> 00:04:30.817
Two more files are optional to the
kit: the tags, and the imported CRLs.

73
00:04:30.817 --> 00:04:36.155
On the O C I side: users, their
credentials, dynamic groups and policies.

74
00:04:36.155 --> 00:04:39.830
You supply a settings file, and
optionally the exception list.

75
00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:44.853
The tag keys are the program's:
system I D, two owners and a sponsor,

76
00:04:44.853 --> 00:04:48.330
one key each, because
AWS tags allow no comma.

77
00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:52.748
One thing isn't yet specified: the O
C I command line's output field names,

78
00:04:52.748 --> 00:04:55.942
so the kit matches a field
however it's spelled.

79
00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:01.290
So what does the code add?

80
00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:04.865
One script and one module,
running one read-only task.

81
00:05:04.865 --> 00:05:08.634
A config file declares the export
commands an operator runs,

82
00:05:08.634 --> 00:05:12.553
every one a generate, get or list,
and the module runs none of them.

83
00:05:12.553 --> 00:05:15.733
A test fails if the
code ever calls the AWS

84
00:05:15.733 --> 00:05:18.914
or O C I command line,
or makes a web request.

85
00:05:18.914 --> 00:05:22.413
Every run writes a folder:
findings, identities, trust,

86
00:05:22.413 --> 00:05:27.341
a credentials list with identifiers
only, never a secret, the evidence rows,

87
00:05:27.341 --> 00:05:32.190
and a manifest with each input's hash
and any optional file it couldn't read.

88
00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:36.324
The validation kit's check, V K
N P I ten, reads the same folder

89
00:05:36.324 --> 00:05:40.803
for the guide's gates; this kit
gives the full findings behind it.

90
00:05:40.803 --> 00:05:45.441
And it changes nothing: the findings
say what to do, and a person does it.

91
00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:51.452
Twenty-six checks in five groups.

92
00:05:51.452 --> 00:05:55.171
AWS long-lived credentials, N
P I A W S oh one to oh seven:

93
00:05:55.171 --> 00:05:59.552
every active key with its age and
last use, two keys on one user,

94
00:05:59.552 --> 00:06:05.072
a key unused for ninety days, which is
design guidance, a password beside a key,

95
00:06:05.072 --> 00:06:09.365
service-specific credentials,
certificates and a root key.

96
00:06:09.365 --> 00:06:14.294
Owners and trust, oh eight to thirteen:
missing tags, O I D C trusts with no

97
00:06:14.294 --> 00:06:19.902
subject condition, outside accounts,
everyone as principal, another partition.

98
00:06:19.902 --> 00:06:22.690
Then trust anchors with no imported CRL.

99
00:06:22.690 --> 00:06:27.403
On O C I, N P I O C I oh one to ten:
every API key, keys past ninety days,

100
00:06:27.403 --> 00:06:31.645
tokens and secret keys that
never expire, missing tags,

101
00:06:31.645 --> 00:06:35.415
and dynamic groups that
reach the whole tenancy.

102
00:06:35.415 --> 00:06:39.355
A user with no long-lived
credential is treated as a person,

103
00:06:39.355 --> 00:06:43.680
or unknown, and skipped by the
owner checks: design guidance.

104
00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:51.605
Six things are still open, and the kit
doesn't assume an answer to any of them.

105
00:06:51.605 --> 00:06:55.617
First, AWS GovCloud and
Entra, in either direction.

106
00:06:55.617 --> 00:06:59.921
AWS documents GovCloud's outbound
federation across its own partitions,

107
00:06:59.921 --> 00:07:04.012
but no page read walks through Entra
and GovCloud trusting each other,

108
00:07:04.012 --> 00:07:06.693
so the guide uses either
path only after a test.

109
00:07:06.693 --> 00:07:11.166
Second, whether Access Analyzer's
unused-access findings run in GovCloud;

110
00:07:11.166 --> 00:07:13.880
the kit's ninety-day check
doesn't depend on it.

111
00:07:13.880 --> 00:07:16.417
Third, whether instance principals,

112
00:07:16.417 --> 00:07:21.405
resource principals and O K E workload
identity are available in O C two.

113
00:07:21.405 --> 00:07:24.955
Fourth, the O C I command
line's output field names.

114
00:07:24.955 --> 00:07:28.567
Fifth, which credential report
column belongs to which key.

115
00:07:28.567 --> 00:07:34.170
And sixth, whether the O C I user list
returns users in every identity domain.

116
00:07:37.970 --> 00:07:39.083
Who's involved?

117
00:07:39.083 --> 00:07:43.577
The cloud engineers who run the exports
in each account and the tenancy, the

118
00:07:43.577 --> 00:07:48.195
identity engineers who own the findings,
and the I S S O, who reads the evidence.

119
00:07:48.195 --> 00:07:50.445
Each output supports a NIST control.

120
00:07:50.445 --> 00:07:55.470
The identity list supports account management,
inventory and identifier management.

121
00:07:55.470 --> 00:07:58.701
The credentials list supports
authenticator management,

122
00:07:58.701 --> 00:08:02.658
and the trust anchors and their CRLs
support public key authentication.

123
00:08:02.658 --> 00:08:04.953
Trust and reach support least privilege,

124
00:08:04.953 --> 00:08:07.720
and the orphan leads
support disabling accounts.

125
00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:10.420
The passing tests support testing changes.

126
00:08:10.420 --> 00:08:13.891
Each row names the fed-ramp
twenty X K S I's aligned with it,

127
00:08:13.891 --> 00:08:16.608
re-read from FedRAMP
on November twentieth.

128
00:08:16.608 --> 00:08:21.357
Two cautions: a row provides evidence
for a control, and the assessor decides.

129
00:08:21.357 --> 00:08:24.214
And K S I's are indicators
for cloud providers;

130
00:08:24.214 --> 00:08:27.370
the agency lines its
evidence up with theirs.

131
00:08:31.170 --> 00:08:35.683
Lab 1 needs PowerShell 7 with
Pester, and no account or tenancy.

132
00:08:35.683 --> 00:08:40.907
The fixtures are one fictional GovCloud
account and one fictional O C two tenancy.

133
00:08:40.907 --> 00:08:45.612
Seven IAM users, one of them a person
and one the root user; eleven roles

134
00:08:45.612 --> 00:08:50.233
and a service-linked one; three O I
D C providers and two trust anchors.

135
00:08:50.233 --> 00:08:55.508
On the O C I side, five users, three
dynamic groups and three policies.

136
00:08:55.508 --> 00:08:57.723
Each is built to trip one or more rules,

137
00:08:57.723 --> 00:09:00.795
and two credentials are
under a recorded exception.

138
00:09:00.795 --> 00:09:02.533
Step 1 runs the checks.

139
00:09:02.533 --> 00:09:07.720
The summary says twenty-four findings,
eleven warnings and two for information;

140
00:09:07.720 --> 00:09:12.594
seven long-lived credentials in AWS and
five in O C I; and nothing left unread.

141
00:09:12.594 --> 00:09:15.630
Then one line per rule,
and the manifest's hash.

142
00:09:19.430 --> 00:09:21.380
Step 2 reads the findings.

143
00:09:21.380 --> 00:09:24.205
Look for the user called
svc report export.

144
00:09:24.205 --> 00:09:26.959
It has two active keys,
one of them never used,

145
00:09:26.959 --> 00:09:30.555
a console password beside them,
and only one owner tagged.

146
00:09:30.555 --> 00:09:33.971
Then the dynamic group called
dg everything: no tags,

147
00:09:33.971 --> 00:09:37.705
no policy names it, and its
rule matches the whole tenancy.

148
00:09:37.705 --> 00:09:42.244
Step 3 reads the long-lived
credentials, one row per active key,

149
00:09:42.244 --> 00:09:46.871
service-specific credential, API
key, auth token and secret key,

150
00:09:46.871 --> 00:09:50.342
with its age, last use for
AWS keys, and exception.

151
00:09:50.342 --> 00:09:53.324
The oldest AWS key is
nine hundred and five

152
00:09:53.324 --> 00:09:56.305
days old and was last
used almost a year ago.

153
00:09:56.305 --> 00:10:00.295
Two credentials are under
exceptions until 2027:

154
00:10:00.295 --> 00:10:04.293
svc app sync's key, and hr
report reader's API key.

155
00:10:08.090 --> 00:10:10.728
Step 4 reads who isn't joined to a record.

156
00:10:10.728 --> 00:10:16.657
Seven identities: two AWS users and two
roles, one of them a role anyone can assume,

157
00:10:16.657 --> 00:10:20.190
two O C I users, and the
dynamic group from before.

158
00:10:20.190 --> 00:10:21.452
Then one trust.

159
00:10:21.452 --> 00:10:25.217
The role called ci pipeline oidc
trusts GitHub's token issuer,

160
00:10:25.217 --> 00:10:29.128
but its only condition is on the
token's audience, not its subject.

161
00:10:29.128 --> 00:10:32.565
So any repository's workflow
could present a token for it.

162
00:10:32.565 --> 00:10:36.992
That's N P I A W S oh nine, and it's
exactly what AWS's own examples

163
00:10:36.992 --> 00:10:42.010
guard against by limiting the
subject claim to one repository.

164
00:10:45.810 --> 00:10:48.511
Step 5 runs without
the two optional files,

165
00:10:48.511 --> 00:10:52.910
as an export made before the guide
added the tag and CRL reads would be.

166
00:10:52.910 --> 00:10:55.679
The kit still runs, and says
what it couldn't check:

167
00:10:55.679 --> 00:10:59.977
the two files are listed as not read,
there's one owner warning for the whole

168
00:10:59.977 --> 00:11:04.698
account instead of a finding per identity,
and a CRL warning for each trust anchor.

169
00:11:04.698 --> 00:11:09.173
With no exception list given, svc
app sync's key is back to a finding.

170
00:11:09.173 --> 00:11:12.874
Step 6 reads the evidence:
one row per step and control,

171
00:11:12.874 --> 00:11:16.072
with its K S I's and the
hash of the file behind it.

172
00:11:16.072 --> 00:11:19.185
Step 7 runs the tests: thirty-four pass.

173
00:11:19.185 --> 00:11:20.758
They sit in their own folder,

174
00:11:20.758 --> 00:11:24.000
so the counts in the other
chapters' labs don't change.

175
00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:32.595
Lab 2 uses one GovCloud account and one
O C I tenancy that aren't production,

176
00:11:32.595 --> 00:11:37.625
with a role or user that can read IAM in
each, and the two command lines signed in.

177
00:11:37.625 --> 00:11:41.062
Nobody has run it yet, so no
step's output has been seen.

178
00:11:41.062 --> 00:11:45.951
Write the settings and an empty exception
list, in a folder outside the repository.

179
00:11:45.951 --> 00:11:51.100
Export the AWS account with the guide's
commands, tags and CRLs included.

180
00:11:51.100 --> 00:11:55.640
Export the tenancy against its home
region; the policy list reads only one

181
00:11:55.640 --> 00:11:58.989
compartment, so add each
child compartment's policies,

182
00:11:58.989 --> 00:12:00.850
or a group will show as unnamed.

183
00:12:00.850 --> 00:12:02.013
Run the checks.

184
00:12:02.013 --> 00:12:06.453
If every O C I user shows no credentials
while the console shows keys,

185
00:12:06.453 --> 00:12:08.912
the field names differ, so record them.

186
00:12:08.912 --> 00:12:12.046
Finally, the manifest should
show no requests at all,

187
00:12:12.046 --> 00:12:14.390
and the fourteen export commands.

188
00:12:18.190 --> 00:12:19.890
Each danger has a guard.

189
00:12:19.890 --> 00:12:23.433
A key beyond the report's first
two, or a CodeCommit password,

190
00:12:23.433 --> 00:12:27.465
would stay out of every review, so
the key lists are required inputs.

191
00:12:27.465 --> 00:12:30.351
If the tag export is skipped,
every role looks fine,

192
00:12:30.351 --> 00:12:33.590
so the kit warns and the
manifest lists the missing file.

193
00:12:33.590 --> 00:12:36.448
An O I D C role that
trusts a whole issuer can

194
00:12:36.448 --> 00:12:39.715
be assumed by any workload
that issuer signs for.

195
00:12:39.715 --> 00:12:44.538
A revoked certificate can keep working
through Roles Anywhere, because GovCloud

196
00:12:44.538 --> 00:12:46.736
can't fetch CRLs from the certificate,

197
00:12:46.736 --> 00:12:49.502
so put CRL imports on
the certificate calendar.

198
00:12:49.502 --> 00:12:52.590
O C I tokens and secret keys never expire.

199
00:12:52.590 --> 00:12:55.452
A dynamic group that matches
the whole tenancy gives

200
00:12:55.452 --> 00:12:58.315
its rights to everyone who
can sign in to any instance.

201
00:12:58.315 --> 00:13:01.253
A new command line version
could change field names.

202
00:13:01.253 --> 00:13:05.170
And exports belong with the
evidence, never in the repository.

203
00:13:08.970 --> 00:13:10.545
Here's what done looks like.

204
00:13:10.545 --> 00:13:13.273
The guide's exports,
tags and CRLs included,

205
00:13:13.273 --> 00:13:17.320
run in every GovCloud account,
and in the tenancy's home region.

206
00:13:17.320 --> 00:13:22.484
Every role, workload user, O C I user
with a credential, and dynamic group

207
00:13:22.484 --> 00:13:27.470
carries a system I D, two owners and
a sponsor, or is in an orphan ticket.

208
00:13:27.470 --> 00:13:30.310
Every long-lived credential
has an exception with

209
00:13:30.310 --> 00:13:32.745
a replacement date, or
a ticket to replace it.

210
00:13:32.745 --> 00:13:37.407
No O C I key is older than ninety
days, and no root access key is active.

211
00:13:37.407 --> 00:13:39.991
Every O I D C trust names its workload,

212
00:13:39.991 --> 00:13:44.296
and every outside or open
principal is removed or justified.

213
00:13:44.296 --> 00:13:46.945
Every trust anchor has an imported CRL.

214
00:13:46.945 --> 00:13:50.295
No dynamic group reaches the
whole tenancy without a reason.

215
00:13:50.295 --> 00:13:54.210
And each run's evidence and manifest
are filed with the change record.
